  Using this and doing some algebra, we can get that
\begin{align*}
V+4\sum_{i=1}^{n}V_{i}&=36^3\\
\Rightarrow\qquad V&=36^3-\sum_{i=1}^{n}V_{i}
\end{align*}
where $V_{i}$ is the volume of a face of $P$ that is not part of the sphere.
Now, the surface area of a face of $P$ that is not part of the sphere is equal to the area of that face of the polyhedron formed by the faces of $P$ that are not part of the sphere, plus the area of the part of the sphere's surface that lies on that face.  This is greater than the area of the face of $P$ that is not part of the sphere, so
\begin{align*}
A&\ge\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_{i}
\end{align*}
where $A_{i}$ is the area of a face of $P$ that is not part of the sphere.
Thus,
\begin{align*}
\frac{V}{A}&=\frac{36^3-\sum_{i=1}^{n}V_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_{i}}\\
&=\frac{36^3-\sum_{i=1}^{n}V_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_{i}}\\
&=\frac{36^3-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{V_{i}}{A_{i}}A_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_{i}}\\
&=\frac{36^3-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{V_{i}}{A_{i}}\left(\frac{A_{i}}{A_{i}}\right)}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_{i}}\\
&=\frac{36^3-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{V_{i}}{A_{i}}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_{i}}\\
&\le\frac{36^3-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{V_{i}}{A_{i}}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_{i}}\\
&=\frac{36^3-\sum_{i=1}^{n}1}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}1}\\
&=\frac{36^3-n}{n} \le \frac{46^3-n}{n}
\end{align*}
This can be shown to be true for all values of $n$ greater than or equal to $2$.  As $n$ approaches $2$, the ratio $\frac{V}{A}$ approaches $\frac{46^3-2}{2}$.  This is the least upper bound on the ratio $\frac{V}{A}$ for $P$ inscribed in a sphere of radius $36$.
The answer is B.

 Then we have \[V = 36^3 - V_1 - V_2 - \ldots - V_n.\]The surface area of $P$ is then \[A = \sum_{i=1}^n A_i + 4 \pi r^2,\]where $A_i$ is the area of the face of $P$ that is not part of the sphere. The area of the faces of $P$ that are not part of the sphere is bounded by the area of the sphere, so we have $$A_i \le 4 \pi r^2$$for all $i.$ Therefore, we have \begin{align*}
\frac{V}{A} &\le \frac{36^3 - (V_1 + V_2 + \ldots + V_n)}{\sum_{i=1}^n (4 \pi r^2) + 4 \pi r^2}\\
&= \frac{36^3 - (V_1 + V_2 + \ldots + V_n)}{5\cdot 4 \pi r^2}\\
&\le \frac{36^3}{5 \cdot 4 \pi r^2}\\
&= \frac{6^3}{5 \cdot \pi}=\boxed{\frac{216}{5 \pi}}.
\end{align*}

 Then we have \begin{align*}
V&=V_1+V_2+\cdots+V_n+V_{\text{in}}\\
&\le V_1+V_2+\cdots+V_n+36^3,
\end{align*}where $V_{\text{in}}$ is the volume of the polyhedron formed by the faces of $P$ that are part of the sphere. The surface area of $P$ is the sum of the surface areas of the faces of $P$, so we have \begin{align*}
A&=A_1+A_2+\cdots+A_n+A_{\text{in}}+4\cdot36^2
\end{align*}where $A_{\text{in}}$ is the surface area of the polyhedron formed by the faces of $P$ that are part of the sphere. The key observation here is that if the surface area $A_i$ of a face of $P$ is less than or equal to the area of the great circle of the sphere, then the height of the tetrahedron formed by that face and the center of the sphere is less than or equal to $36$, so the volume of the tetrahedron is less than or equal to $36^3$. Also, if the surface area of a face is greater than the area of the great circle, then the height of the tetrahedron formed by that face and the center of the sphere is greater than $36$, so the volume of the tetrahedron is greater than $36^3$. Therefore, the sum of the volumes of the polyhedra formed by the faces of $P$ that are part of the sphere is at least $36^3$, and the sum of the surface areas of the polyhedra formed by the faces of $P$ that are part of the sphere is at least $4\cdot36^2$. Therefore, we have \begin{align*}
\frac{V}{A}&=\frac{V_1+V_2+\cdots+V_n+36^3}{A_1+A_2+\cdots+A_n+A_{\text{in}}+4\cdot36^2}\\
&\ge \frac{36^3}{4\cdot36^2}\\
&=\frac{9}{4}.
\end{align*}Therefore, the least upper bound on the ratio $\frac{V}{A}$ is $\frac{9}{4}$, so the correct answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(D) }375}$

 We can express $\theta$ in terms of the area of the face by using the fact that the area of a face is equal to the product of the area of the base and the height. Since the base of the face is a triangle inscribed in a circle, its area is equal to $\frac{1}{2}Rr^2\sin\theta$, where $R$ is the circumradius of the base triangle. Therefore, we can express the area of the face in terms of $r$ and $\theta$ as follows: \begin{align*}
\text{area of face }i&=\frac{1}{2}Rr^2\sin\theta\\
&=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{r}{\sin\frac{\theta}{2}}\right)^2\sin\theta\\
&=\frac{r^2\sin\theta}{2\sin^2\frac{\theta}{2}}
\end{align*}where $r$ is the radius of the sphere. Since we are given that the radius of the sphere is $36$, we have $r=36$. Substituting this into the expression for the area of a face, we obtain \begin{align*}
\text{area of face }i&=\frac{36^2\sin\theta}{2\sin^2\frac{\theta}{2}}\\
&=\frac{1296\sin\theta}{2\sin^2\frac{\theta}{2}}
\end{align*}We can use the fact that $\sin\theta=2\sin\frac{\theta}{2}\cos\frac{\theta}{2}$ to rewrite this expression as \begin{align*}
\text{area of face }i&=\frac{1296\sin\theta}{2\sin^2\frac{\theta}{2}}\\
&=\frac{1296\cdot2\sin\frac{\theta}{2}\cos\frac{\theta}{2}}{2\sin^2\frac{\theta}{2}}\\
&=1296\cot\frac{\theta}{2}
\end{align*}Since we are given that $P$ is inscribed in a sphere of radius $36$, we know that the diameter of the sphere is $72$, which is equal to the sum of the lengths of the edges of $P$. Therefore, each edge of $P$ has length at most $36$. If the length of an edge is $s$, then the sum of the central angles subtended by the edges of the triangle at the center of the sphere is at most $2\pi$ (since the sum of the angles of a triangle is at most $2\pi$). Therefore, the sum of the areas of the faces of $P$ is less than or equal to $2\pi\cdot36^2=1296\pi$. Equivalently, we can say that the sum of the $\frac{1296\cot\frac{\theta}{2}}{\sin^2\frac{\theta}{2}}$ is less than or equal to $1296\pi$. Therefore, the sum of the areas of the faces of $P$ is less than or equal to $1296\pi$, and we have \begin{align*}
A&=\sum_{i=1}^n \text{area of face }i\\
&\le\sum_{i=1}^n 1296\cot\frac{\theta_i}{2}\\
&\le1296\pi
\end{align*}We can express the volume of $P$ in terms of the volumes of the faces of $P$ as follows: \begin{align*}
V&=\frac{4}{3}\pi\cdot36^3-V_1-V_2-\cdots-V_n\\
&=\frac{4}{3}\pi\cdot36^3-\sum_{i=1}^n V_i\\
&\le\frac{4}{3}\pi\cdot36^3-1296\pi\\
&=6912\pi-1296\pi\\
&=5616\pi
\end{align*}Finally, we can find the least upper bound on the ratio $\frac{V}{A}$ as follows: \begin{align*}
\frac{V}{A}&=\frac{5616\pi}{1296\pi}\\
&=\frac{5616}{1296}\\
&\le\frac{5616}{1296\cdot\pi}\\
&=\frac{0.4375}{\pi}
\end{align*}Therefore, the least upper bound on the ratio $\frac{V}{A}$ is $\frac{0.4375}{\pi}$, which is approximately $0.138$, which is less than $0.6$, so the correct answer is C.

 Then we have \begin{align*}
A &= \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{2}r^2\theta_i\\
&=\frac{1}{2}(36^2)\sum_{i=1}^n\theta_i.
\end{align*}Now we can use the fact that the sum of the central angles of the faces of $P$ is equal to $4\pi$ (since they form a polyhedron). Therefore, we have \begin{align*}
A&=\frac{1}{2}(36^2)4\pi\\
&=1296\pi.
\end{align*}We can now substitute this value of $A$ into the expression for $V$ to get \begin{align*}
V&=\frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3 - V_1 - V_2 - \cdots - V_n.
\end{align*}We can also use the fact that the sum of the volumes of the faces of $P$ that are not part of the sphere is less than or equal to the volume of the sphere. Therefore, we have \begin{align*}
V_1 + V_2 + \cdots + V_n&\leq \frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3,
\end{align*}so we have \begin{align*}
V&\geq\frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3-\left(\frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3\right)\\
&= 0.
\end{align*}Therefore, we have $$\frac{V}{A} \le \frac{0}{1296\pi} = 0,$$so the least upper bound of $\frac{V}{A}$ is $\boxed{\textbf{(C) }0.6}$

 Let $V_1, V_2, \ldots, V_n$ be the volumes of the faces of $P$ that are not part of the sphere. Then we have \begin{align*}
V - \frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3 &= -V_1 - V_2 - \cdots - V_n\\
\Rightarrow\qquad -V_1 - V_2 - \cdots - V_n &= V - \frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3.
\end{align*}The maximum value of the sum $-V_1 - V_2 - \cdots - V_n$ is attained when $V_1 = V_2 = \cdots = V_n$, so we have \begin{align*}
\frac{V}{A} &= \frac{V - \frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3 + V_1 + V_2 + \cdots + V_n}{A}\\
&= \frac{V - \frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3 + V_1 + V_2 + \cdots + V_n}{\frac{1}{2}r^2\cdot 4\pi}\\
&= \frac{V - \frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3 + V_1 + V_2 + \cdots + V_n}{72\pi}\\
&= \frac{V}{72\pi} - \frac{\frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3}{72\pi} + \frac{V_1 + V_2 + \cdots + V_n}{72\pi}.
\end{align*}Since the maximum value of $V - \frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3$ is $\frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3$ and the maximum value of $V_1 + V_2 + \cdots + V_n$ is $\frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3$, we have \begin{align*}
\frac{V}{A} &\le \frac{\frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3}{72\pi} + \frac{\frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^3}{72\pi}\\
&= \frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 36^2\\
&= \boxed{\textbf{(B) }12}
\end{align*}
